  <title>Rebuildable Airflow: snapshot the running container, redeploy with a rollback</title>
  <link>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-11-11-airflow-image-snapshot-and-redeploy/</link>
  <pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 17:04:23 &#43;0800</pubDate>
  <author>william</author>
  <guid>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-11-11-airflow-image-snapshot-and-redeploy/</guid>
  <description><![CDATA[<div class="featured-image"><img loading="eager" src="https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-11-11-airflow-image-snapshot-and-redeploy/featured-image.png" alt="Rebuildable Airflow: snapshot the running container, redeploy with a rollback" title="Rebuildable Airflow: snapshot the running container, redeploy with a rollback" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"/></div><blockquote>
<p><strong>Update 2026-11-11 17:30:</strong> image tags are now the build time, <code>yyyymmddTHHMMSS</code> (e.g. <code>airflow:20261111T172041</code>), so two builds on the same day no longer collide, and <code>./run.sh</code> with no tag lists what&rsquo;s available. The outputs below are from the first build, which still used a plain date tag, <code>20261111</code>. The downloadable scripts are the current versions.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The <a href="/2026-11-11-airflow-outage-postmortem/">outage postmortem</a> had one recurring theme: every time someone rebuilt the Airflow container by hand, it came back missing something. A mount, the concurrency settings, the plugin, the registry login. The image was whatever someone last typed, and the settings that mattered lived only in a running container.</p>
<p>So the same afternoon I wrote three small scripts. <code>build.airflow.sh</code> turns the running container into an image tagged with its build time (<code>airflow:20261111T172041</code>), pushes it, and saves a tarball. <code>run.sh</code> recreates the container from that tag and checks it&rsquo;s actually healthy, or puts the old one back. <code>restart.sh</code> runs inside the container and keeps the scheduler and webserver up. <strong>From the 170 entries in <code>docker diff</code>, the image keeps 13 files (172K). A redeploy is healthy about 15 s after the new container starts, and a failed one rolls back in 1 s.</strong></p>]]></description>
</item>
<item>
  <title>One night, one dead disk: an Airflow trading-host outage and what it taught me</title>
  <link>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-11-11-airflow-outage-postmortem/</link>
  <pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:48:10 &#43;0800</pubDate>
  <author>william</author>
  <guid>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-11-11-airflow-outage-postmortem/</guid>
  <description><![CDATA[<div class="featured-image"><img loading="eager" src="https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-11-11-airflow-outage-postmortem/featured-image.png" alt="One night, one dead disk: an Airflow trading-host outage and what it taught me" title="One night, one dead disk: an Airflow trading-host outage and what it taught me" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"/></div><blockquote>
<p><strong>Update 2026-11-11:</strong> the &ldquo;a restart is a procedure, not a command&rdquo; advice below is now three scripts: a dated image built from the running container, a redeploy that refuses while tasks run and rolls back on failure, and a supervisor inside the container. See <a href="/2026-11-11-airflow-image-snapshot-and-redeploy/">Rebuildable Airflow</a>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>At 19:56 one evening, a 21.8 TB data disk on our Airflow host started throwing I/O errors. By 21:37 someone had pointed Docker at a fresh, empty data directory to get things running again. That one change, made under pressure and never written down, turned into <strong>five separate incidents by 09:30 the next morning</strong>: a message broker that vanished, a missing mount, concurrency limits back at their defaults, live-trading processes Airflow no longer knew about, and two restarts that orphaned every live trade.</p>
<p>Everything was back by 10:08. This post is the timeline, the root cause of each problem, how each was fixed (including the 1.5-minute outage I caused myself while fixing it), and what I would change so this can&rsquo;t happen the same way again.</p>]]></description>
</item>
<item>
  <title>Use the IDC proxy from XPS and Codex</title>
  <link>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-11-06-idc-v2raya-proxy-codex/</link>
  <pubDate>Fri, 06 Nov 2026 13:00:00 &#43;0800</pubDate>
  <author>william</author>
  <guid>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-11-06-idc-v2raya-proxy-codex/</guid>
  <description><![CDATA[<blockquote>
<p><strong>Update 2026-11-06:</strong> a later startup investigation found a separate delay. Different Codex versions rejected each other&rsquo;s shared model cache and triggered model-list refreshes that timed out after five seconds. See <a href="/2026-11-06-codex-startup-model-cache/">Codex startup and the shared model cache</a>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My XPS uses <code>lfang.idc</code> as a proxy gateway. v2rayA runs on the IDC host, and applications on XPS connect to the gateway over the private network. Codex needs one extra check: its shared app-server daemon keeps the environment it received when it started.</p>]]></description>
</item>
<item>
  <title>docker buildx: the builder had a proxy, the client didn&#39;t</title>
  <link>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-10-16-docker-buildx-anonymous-token-timeout/</link>
  <pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 14:03:28 &#43;0900</pubDate>
  <author>william</author>
  <guid>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-10-16-docker-buildx-anonymous-token-timeout/</guid>
  <description><![CDATA[<p>My image build script started failing on its very first line, <code>FROM python:3.10</code>,
with <code>failed to fetch anonymous token ... TLS handshake timeout</code>. The odd part:
the buildx builder I use is created with a proxy for exactly this reason, and
from another shell the same build worked.</p>
<p><strong>The token request is not made by the builder. It is made by the <code>docker buildx</code>
client in your own shell, using your shell&rsquo;s proxy variables.</strong> My interactive
zsh exports none, so that one request went direct and timed out. Two <code>export</code>
lines in the build script fixed it.</p>]]></description>
</item>
